complete -c netrune -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a dashboard -d 'Print every dashboards URL and say whether the grapher is answering. save keeps a dashboard you have rearranged in the browser, writing it in place of the generated one so it survives the next stack init; revert goes back to the generated version. Dashboards are generated because a generator keeps five of them consistent and hand-edited JSON does not, but a board you have arranged the way you read it should not have to be given up for that. --open launches the overview in a browser. When it is not answering this says which of the two reasons it is -- the service is not started, or the machines per-user systemd manager is not running at all -- because from a browser those look identical and lead somewhere quite different.'
complete -c netrune -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a health -d 'Show switch-level readings for every switch with a watched port: uptime, control-plane CPU, memory, temperature, and open sessions. These are collected automatically alongside the ports, because a port that went quiet reads differently depending on whether its switch rebooted or ran out of CPU. A dash means the switch does not answer that OID; nothing is wrong. Failed SSH logins are in no standard MIB -- read the switchs syslog for those.'
