complete -c netrune -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a exporter -d 'Publish collected readings as Prometheus exposition for VictoriaMetrics to scrape. Counters are raw and monotonic; rates are derived at query time. Binds loopback, answers GET only, and runs no command. --once prints the body and exits.'
complete -c netrune -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a backfill -d 'Import history collected before the metrics stack existed into VictoriaMetrics. Stored rates are published as netrune_history_* gauges, kept separate from the live counter series so a derived value is never read as a measured one.'
complete -c netrune -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a stack -d 'Write the VictoriaMetrics scrape config, vmalert rules, dashboards for the selected grapher, systemd user units, and a compose file. Dashboards are provisioned from these files rather than edited in a browser. graphers lists which are available. --role collector generates the collector alone -- no exposition, no storage, no dashboards -- which is what a remote poller needs: the machine that pulls from it keeps and draws the readings, and anything published on the poller would be a new listener on the network it was placed there to observe.'
complete -c netrune -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a export -d 'Print this collectors stored readings as JSONL for another install to pull. Read-only, and limited to what this collector measured: a poller does not pass on what it pulled from somewhere else.'
complete -c netrune -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a pull -d 'Merge a pollers readings into this install. --remote fetches them over SSH; --file merges an export the poller wrote, which is what to use where SSH between the two subnets is not permitted -- an export is a file, and anything that can move a file will do. - reads standard input. A file is merged whole rather than by window, and merging is idempotent, so re-reading one adds nothing. Without --since a fetch resumes from the newest reading already merged, so a repeated pull carries only what is new.'
